WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.040
Speaker 1: I saw a super interesting headline&nbsp;
this morning about the U.S. military. And we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:08.880
talked about the U.S. military so much on&nbsp;
this program. It relates to foreign policy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:14.220
it relates to budgetary priorities. It relates&nbsp;
to culture in many ways because there is this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:18.660
military culture that exists in the United States.&nbsp;
Okay. So what's the headline? The headline from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:24.960
the Army Times is that the Army has missed its&nbsp;
recruiting goal by about 15,000 soldiers. And as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:30.300
you read through the article by Lolita Baldor&nbsp;
from the Associated Press, you see that that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:37.200
15,000 soldier shortfall is about 25%&nbsp;
of its recruitment goal this year. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:43.320
at big companies, when you miss earnings&nbsp;
projections by 2%, it's a pretty big deal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:49.140
So understand that falling 25% short and&nbsp;
the military is a large organization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:56.220
falling 25% short of your recruitment goal&nbsp;
for a year is a really, really big deal. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:01.620
article also points out that all military services&nbsp;
struggled in what they're describing as a tight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:09.900
jobs market to find young people both willing&nbsp;
and fit to enlist, adding while the Army was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:16.080
the only service that didn't meet its target, all&nbsp;
others had to dig deep into their pool of delayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:22.740
entry applicants, which will put them behind as&nbsp;
they begin the next recruiting year on Saturday.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:30.420
The article, to its credit, says the worsening&nbsp;
problem stirs debate about whether America's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:35.580
fighting force should be restructured or reduced&nbsp;
in size if the services can't recruit enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:40.260
and also puts added pressure on National Guard&nbsp;
and Reserves to help meet mission requirements.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:44.820
So there's actually a bunch of interesting&nbsp;
layers to this conversation. First of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:50.820
is just the question why aren't as many people&nbsp;
as at times past applying, or are the people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:58.860
applying not meaning the relatively modest but&nbsp;
but still their requirements for joining the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:03.600
military when it comes to physical fitness and&nbsp;
health conditions and this sort of thing? One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:11.460
really interesting aspect to this is that wages&nbsp;
are up in many sectors, including sectors that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:16.740
could be alternatives to joining the military.&nbsp;
And if you look at wage and salary growth in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:22.500
the United States, this is a chart looking at the&nbsp;
last 25 years. You see that basically dating back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:29.280
to sort of early in 2020, the pandemic, quite&nbsp;
frankly, there has been significant wage growth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:34.020
in the United States. Now there are those who will&nbsp;
say this is a good thing because people are making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:40.140
more. Some on the right who often criticize&nbsp;
lack of wage growth when there's a Democratic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:43.980
president now say the wage growth is bad because&nbsp;
it's part of inflation, which is making it hard to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:47.820
hire people. The rate is going to say it's bad&nbsp;
no matter what if a Democrat is in office. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:55.260
let's be quite frank and direct about this. If you&nbsp;
can make a better wage not joining the military,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.260 --> 00:03:02.340
there are lots of people who will choose to do the&nbsp;
job outside of the military or said a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:09.720
way. When wages aren't as oppressively low outside&nbsp;
of the military, it's going to be bad for military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:17.100
recruiting. Now, there's also sort of political&nbsp;
reasons why folks might not be jumping to sign up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:22.500
for the military at this point in time. Look at&nbsp;
someone like Republican Congressman Matt Gaetz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:27.780
who over the last week has been in a bunch of&nbsp;
viral video clips saying, why don't we get rid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:34.380
of the VA altogether, the Veterans Affairs&nbsp;
Department. And of course, you might believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:41.940
that he really wants to improve care for veterans&nbsp;
by getting rid of the VA and doing it a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:48.600
way. But for the most part, veterans like the VA&nbsp;
and it's probably not helping recruiting that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:55.920
have even Republicans who are supposedly always&nbsp;
for the military and veterans openly hostile to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:02.520
some of the aspects of the military world, which&nbsp;
includes the VA and saying we should we should get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:09.540
rid of that. We have the stories of female service&nbsp;
members regularly assaulted and worried that if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:16.020
they report the assault within the military, they&nbsp;
will be sent for a psych eval, may be deemed unfit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:21.780
for service, and they are the ones who who will&nbsp;
suffer rather than the person they accuse of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:28.080
assaulting them. But that's just the kind of nitty&nbsp;
gritty of why might we be seeing this problem. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:36.420
of course, you can also make the claim, although&nbsp;
I don't have data on it, that maybe. People who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:45.180
are targeted by the military to join are seeing it&nbsp;
as a valiant mission compared to at times past. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:50.040
know recruiting after 911 went sky high because so&nbsp;
many people thought, if I join the military now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:57.960
I'm going to be able to do something to stop or&nbsp;
hurt the people that did. 911 Then they were sent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:01.980
to Iraq, which had nothing to do with 911, and&nbsp;
the rest is history. But that's not the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:06.960
for today. But there's a whole other aspect to&nbsp;
this, which is it should make us ask the question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:15.120
why does the military need to be so big? And we&nbsp;
can talk about big in terms of what it costs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:23.280
but also huge militaries can cause foreign policy&nbsp;
blunders. And we've seen this in the past. If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:29.520
look at stories from the Korean War, Vietnam to&nbsp;
some degree, when you have a really big military,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:37.920
it can make one believe that it is even more&nbsp;
powerful and invulnerable. And you make military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:43.200
decisions that go above and beyond even the&nbsp;
power that you have. And it can lead to very bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:49.980
military decision. Decisions create quagmires of&nbsp;
endless, sort of like a constant rotation of loss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:54.660
of life. It's kind of what's happening to the&nbsp;
Russian military in Ukraine right now. They do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.660 --> 00:06:01.020
have the stronger military, but they overstated in&nbsp;
their own minds even the strength. And so there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:05.880
lots of geopolitical reasons why having a big&nbsp;
military isn't necessarily the best thing. And of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:11.520
course, if you're someone like me, a progressive&nbsp;
social Democrat, I want I'm not for abolishing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:17.340
military, but I recognize there are threats to the&nbsp;
United States. The U.S. should have a military,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:23.160
but it shouldn't be nearly as large and far&nbsp;
reaching as the one we have is. And so much of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:28.380
the money that is spent on defense could be better&nbsp;
spent elsewhere. I won't give you the full rant of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:34.800
all the areas in which it could be spent, but I've&nbsp;
many clips and stories about that. So needless to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:41.940
say, the military struggling to recruit what comes&nbsp;
next. And to be clear, this has nothing to do with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:48.300
Joe Biden. There are I saw anecdotally on Twitter&nbsp;
people saying this is because Biden has disgraced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:53.580
the country so no one wants to serve. There's no&nbsp;
evidence of that whatsoever. I also saw this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:58.200
because of Joe Biden's student loan debt relief,&nbsp;
because one of the reasons people were joining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:02.580
the military was because they needed money&nbsp;
to pay off their student loans. First of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:08.880
that debt relief was only just announced and that&nbsp;
couldn't possibly be the reason why in the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:13.200
year there was a shortfall in recruiting.&nbsp;
And number two, you're sort of saying like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:17.340
we need to keep people really desperate&nbsp;
financially so that they'll join the military.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:22.320
That's not really that doesn't speak wonders&nbsp;
about the American military, if that's what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:26.460
it takes. Let me know your thoughts. I don't know&nbsp;
that this problem is going to abate any time soon

